# XSH 2.1 Use and Implementation of Functions
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# General rules for functions and macros that every page relies on. Facts
# a page already states for its own function are not repeated here.

assertion  XSH.2.1-use-of-functions.001
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.1
class      required
says       Each of getc, putc, getchar, putchar, feof, ferror, clearerr, fileno,
           getc_unlocked, putc_unlocked, getchar_unlocked, putchar_unlocked,
           isalpha, isdigit, toupper, tolower, abs and labs can be called with
           its name in parentheses (which suppresses a macro of that name), and
           its address can be taken and used for a call, with the right
           results.
test       t/001-suppress-and-address.c

assertion  XSH.2.1-use-of-functions.002
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.1
class      required
says       After #undef of each name in the list of 001, the name still
           refers to a working function: it can be called and its address
           used.
test       t/002-undef.c

assertion  XSH.2.1-use-of-functions.003
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.1
class      required
says       Calls of feof, ferror, clearerr, fileno, isalpha, isdigit, toupper,
           tolower, abs and labs, and the byte argument of putc and
           putc_unlocked, with arguments that have side effects (an
           increment, a decrement or a comma expression) evaluate each
           argument exactly once, and arguments built with a conditional or
           an assignment expression are taken as a whole.
note       The pages of getc, putc, getc_unlocked and putc_unlocked say a
           macro form may evaluate the stream argument more than once, so
           that argument is always side-effect free here.
test       t/003-args-once.c

assertion  XSH.2.1-use-of-functions.004
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.1
class      required
says       Without including any header, a program that declares abs,
           labs, atoi, atof, rand, srand, toupper, tolower, isalpha,
           isdigit, strcmp, strcpy and sprintf itself
           (using only types that need no header) can call them, with
           right results.
note       Only functions whose prototypes use no type from a header are
           declared. sprintf is declared with its usual
           prototype (char *, const char *, ...).
test       t/004-declare-without-header.c

assertion  XSH.2.1-use-of-functions.005
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.1
class      required
says       Each of EOF, BUFSIZ, RAND_MAX, EXIT_FAILURE, INT_MAX, INT_MIN,
           UINT_MAX, LONG_MAX, LONG_MIN, CHAR_BIT, SIGINT, SIGKILL, EINVAL,
           ENOENT, SEEK_END, FOPEN_MAX, S_IRWXU, O_CREAT and _POSIX_VERSION,
           used unparenthesized in an arithmetic expression, acts as one
           operand: the expression has the value it has with the macro's
           value in a variable.
note       Each macro is combined with *, /, % and - (the large ones only
           with / and %, so nothing overflows), then compared with the same
           expression on a variable that holds the macro's value.
test       t/005-object-macro-grouping.c

assertion  XSH.2.1-use-of-functions.006
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.1
class      required
says       EOF, BUFSIZ, RAND_MAX, EXIT_FAILURE, EXIT_SUCCESS, INT_MAX, INT_MIN,
           UINT_MAX, LONG_MAX, LONG_MIN, CHAR_BIT, SIGINT, SIGKILL, SIGTERM,
           EINVAL, ENOENT, SEEK_SET, SEEK_CUR, SEEK_END and FOPEN_MAX can
           be used in #if directives; EOF is negative there, and the signal
           numbers, error numbers, BUFSIZ and FOPEN_MAX are positive.
note       Only the use in #if and the signs are required here; the
           values of the other constants are tested with their headers.
test       t/006-if-usable.c

assertion  XSH.2.1-use-of-functions.007
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.1
class      required
says       Four threads at once, each calling snprintf, strtol, strtod,
           qsort, bsearch, memcpy, memcmp, strlen, gmtime_r,
           strftime, toupper, regcomp and regexec, fnmatch, mbrtowc and
           strerror_r on data of their own, always get the results the same
           calls give one thread at a time.
note       A race between the calls shows up as a wrong result. The
           functions listed are all required to be thread-safe by XSH 2.9.1.
timeout    60
test       t/007-concurrent-private.c

assertion  XSH.2.1-use-of-functions.008
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.1
class      required
says       The comparison function of qsort() and of bsearch(), the routine of
           pthread_once(), and a signal handler run by raise(), all run in
           the thread that made the call, both in the main thread and in
           another thread.
note       These are operations whose effects the application can see.
test       t/008-callbacks-in-calling-thread.c
